#lang saffron
;; any-widen-of-a-borrowed-local -- a widen COPIES a header; the copy's children
;; are still the original's, and two owners then free them.
;;
;; `(:: inner any)` mallocs a fresh box and copies `inner`'s two words into it.
;; The box is fresh, so storing it in an `:any` field and letting the field's
;; owner free it is right.  But the copy's `tl` word still points at the `(Nil)`
;; box that `inner`'s OWN spine owns -- and since `__tur_any_drop` went deep
;; (2026-09-14) the field owner's `drop_localowned_` walks straight through the
;; copy into it.  `inner`'s scope-exit drop then frees the same box a second
;; time: `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`, on six lines, in both
;; dialects.
;;
;; The localowned drop already had the right frame -- "a local that was moved
;; into a call, moved during its own initialisation, or explicitly consumed has
;; handed ownership on, and the new owner's scope frees the spine instead" -- and
;; a widen is a hand-off that was simply not on the list.  It is now, so `inner`
;; keeps no drop of its own and `outer` releases the spine exactly once.
;;
;; "Stored" is the load-bearing half of the new guard.  A widen in plain ARGUMENT
;; position hands the box to a callee that reads it and returns; nothing outlives
;; the call holding it, so the binding's drop is still needed and still correct.
;; Keying on the widen alone cost `saffron-higher-order` its whole list spine --
;; back from 13 allocations to 17 -- which is why the guard asks whether the
;; CONSUMER's result is itself an aggregate carrying drop glue.  `each-len`
;; below is the argument-position case that must keep its drop.
;;
;; requires.leak-check with no `known-leak`: both halves have to hold at once.
;; Drop twice and ASan aborts; drop never and the spine shows up as leaked.

(defdata Lst [] (Cons [hd : any tl : any]) (Nil))

(defn llen [xs] (match xs (Cons h t) (+ 1 (cast (llen t) int)) (Nil) 0))

(defn drive [i : int n : int] : int
  (if (= i n)
    0
    (let [inner (Cons 1 (Cons 2 (Nil)))
          ;; STORED: `inner` is widened into an `:any` field `outer` owns.
          outer (Cons inner (Nil))]
      (+ (+ (cast (llen inner) int)
            ;; ARGUMENT position: `inner` is read by a callee that returns an
            ;; int.  `inner` must keep its own drop, or its spine leaks.
            (cast (llen outer) int))
         (drive (+ i 1) n)))))

(defn main [] : int
  ;; 2 per `inner`, 1 per `outer`, over 300 turns.
  (println (drive 0 300))
  0)
